  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on stated preference surveys from a specific demographic of university students, which may not reflect the actual purchasing behavior or preferences of the broader urban population.",
      "what": "This study analyzes university students' preferences for Mobility as a Service (MaaS) bundles using stated preference surveys from four European universities in Budapest, Delft, Catania, and Katowice. A mixed logit model was applied to the survey data to identify how existing travel habits and income levels influence the desired composition of transit, cycling, and car-sharing bundles.",
      "why": "The findings demonstrate that MaaS adoption is highly context-dependent, showing that students in cycling-heavy cities reject bike-sharing bundles while those in car-dominated cities require car-sharing options. This provides a framework for customizing MaaS bundles to specific local travel cultures rather than relying on a one-size-fits-all regional strategy."
    }
  },
